WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:07.582
Let's see if we can
multiply 9 times 0.6.

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:14.052
Or another way to write it, we
want to calculate 9 times 0.6.

00:00:14.052 --> 00:00:16.260
I'll write it like this-- 0.6.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.120
We want to figure out
what this is equal to.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.704
And I encourage you
to pause the video

00:00:21.704 --> 00:00:23.245
and try to figure
it out on your own.

00:00:23.245 --> 00:00:25.204
And I'll give you a
little bit of a hint.

00:00:25.204 --> 00:00:34.740
0.6 is the same thing
as 6 divided by 10.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.880
We know that if we start with
6, which we could write as 6.0,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.930
and if you were to divide
it by 10, dividing by 10

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:44.880
is equivalent to moving
the decimal place one

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.750
place to the left.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.220
So 6 divided by 10 is 0.6.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.730
We are moving the decimal
one place to the left.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:56.890
So I'm assuming you
given a go at it.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:59.420
But what I'm going
to do is use this

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.540
that we already know to rewrite
what we're trying to multiply.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.650
So 9 times 0.6 is the
same thing is 9 times--

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:14.350
0.6 is 6 divided by 10.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.270
And this expression
right over here,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.430
we could either do the 6 divided
by 10 first, in which case

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.790
we would get 0.6, and this
would turn into this problem.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:28.030
Or we could do the
9 times 6 first.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.670
And so let's do 9 times 6,
which we know how to calculate,

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.901
and then divide by 10, which
we also know how to do.

00:01:33.901 --> 00:01:34.400
That.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.620
all about just moving
the decimal place.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.130
So we could write 9 times 6.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.218
9 times 6, we
already know, is 54.

00:01:45.218 --> 00:01:48.760
I'll do that in orange--
is going to be 54.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.480
So this right over here is 54.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.610
And now to get to
this expression,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.310
we have to divide by 10.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.295
We have to divide by 10.

00:01:58.295 --> 00:02:00.830
And what happens when we
divide something by 10?

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.520
And we've seen this in previous
videos, why this is the case.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.330
This is all about what
decimal notation means.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:10.889
Each place represents 10
times as much as the place

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:14.310
to its right, or each place
represents 1/10 of the place

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:14.855
to the left.

00:02:14.855 --> 00:02:18.290
So 54 divided by
10, this is going

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.154
to be-- you could start with 54.

00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:25.250
And I'll put a 0 here
after the decimal.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.050
And when you divide
by 10, that's

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.720
equivalent of shifting the
decimal one to the left.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.091
This is going to
be equal to 5.4.

00:02:32.091 --> 00:02:33.760
And that should
make sense to you.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.310
5 times 10 is 50.

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.950
0.4 times 10 is 4.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:42.924
So it makes sense that
54 divided by 10--

00:02:42.924 --> 00:02:43.840
I shouldn't say equal.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.705
I'd write 54 divided
by 10 is equal to 5.4.

00:02:50.705 --> 00:02:54.940
So this right over
here is equal to 5.4,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.820
and that's what this is.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.660
This is equal to 5.4.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.910
Notice, 9 times 6 is 54.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:08.250
9 times 0.6 is 5.4.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.500
Now you might see a
little pattern here.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.690
Between these two numbers,
I had exactly one number

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.000
to the right of the decimal.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.870
When I take its product, let's
say I ignored the decimal.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.290
I just said 9 times 6,
I would've gotten 54.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.570
But then I have to
divide by 10 in order

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.270
to take account of
the decimal, take

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.010
account of the fact
this wasn't a 6.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.152
This was a 6/10.

00:03:30.152 --> 00:03:32.989
And so I have one number to
the right of the decimal here.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:34.530
And I want to you
to think about that

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.160
whether that's a
general principle.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.300
Can we just count the
total numbers of digits

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.210
to the right of the
decimals and then

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:44.800
our product is going to have
the same number of digits

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.390
to right of the decimal?

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.210
I'll let you to
think about that.

